            <ArticleTitle>Multifocal dural enhancement associated with temporal arteritis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>119-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the association of temporal arteritis and enhancement of the dura mater and temporalis muscle.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A single patient with a complaint of headache and diplopia was studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Academic medical center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 69-year-old man presented with lateral rectus weakness, temporal artery tenderness, and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 65 mm/h.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Biopsy of temporal artery and dura mater.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Brain magnetic resonance imaging and pathological findings.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain showed multifocal dural enhancement and enhancement of the temporalis muscles. The temporal artery showed a necrotizing vasculitis and the dura showed perivascular inflammatory cells.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is proposed that the temporal arteritis caused the multifocal dural enhancement and temporalis muscle enhancement on magnetic resonance imaging.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
